            <ArticleTitle>Determining respiratory system resistance and reactance by impulse oscillometry in obese individuals.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>422-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1590/S1806-37132015000004517</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1806-37132015000500422</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate peripheral respiratory system resistance and reactance (Rrs and Xrs, respectively) in obese individuals.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We recruited 99 individuals, dividing them into four groups by body mass index (BMI): &lt; 30.0 kg/m2 (control, n = 31); 30.0-39.9 kg/m2 (obesity, n = 13); 40.0-49.9 kg/m2 (severe obesity, n = 28); and ≥ 50.0 kg/m2 (morbid obesity, n = 13). Using impulse oscillometry, we measured total Rrs, central Rrs, and Xrs. Peripheral Rrs was calculated as the difference between total Rrs and central Rrs. All subjects also underwent spirometry.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Of the 99 individuals recruited, 14 were excluded because they failed to perform forced expiratory maneuvers correctly during spirometry. The individuals in the severe obesity and morbid obesity groups showed higher peripheral Rrs and lower Xrs in comparison with those in the two other groups.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Having a BMI ≥ 40 kg/m2 was associated with a significant increase in peripheral Rrs and with a decrease in Xrs.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
